From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pokeplatinum-3ds <noreply@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 08:00:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] The lower menu as quick access (SDK_PORT_3DS)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

On the 3DS remaster's lower menu, a tap on a button does what it says at
once, leaving what the menu opened leaves the menu too, and the panel
fades to black and back whenever what it shows changes:

- A tap opens the menu straight on the button (its cursor on it from the
  start) with the press's sound (the menu's opening sound is X's:
  field_control.c asks StartMenu_PortTapOpening). What the menu opened,
  left (B, or the bag's button again; the party, the trainer card, the
  Pokédex, the options, the Town Map, the save's question answered no),
  leaves the menu: back to walking, whether a tap or X opened it.
- The panel shows the tab chosen, the map or the Pokétch. Changing tabs
  (MAP, POKéTCH) it fades to black, shows the other one and fades back in
  (NitroPort_LowerMenuFadeToPanel: the port makes the change once the
  panel's black, whatever the game's running then; the Pokétch's picture
  drawn first).
- BAG: the panel fades to black, the menu kept on
  (NitroPort_LowerMenuKeepShown), and the field goes and the bag comes
  behind it, the upper screen keeping the field; the bag pops in once it's
  whole. B or BAG: the panel fades to black again, the field comes back
  behind it, and the panel fades in on the map (the Pokétch for POKéTCH,
  or without the Town Map). The bag's other buttons as before (apps
  straight from it, SAVE back in the field).
- MAP shows the map; only a double tap on it (NitroPort_LowerMenuPressDouble)
  opens the Town Map app, or A on it with the map shown in the menu opened
  with X. A tap on the map does nothing.
- The map told to the port cheaply as the player walks (the port draws it
  off the game's thread): the Town Map's context worked out each time
  with nothing read (town_map/context.c keeps the description flags'
  file, read once), its blocks and the texts' palettes read once, the
  name and the description printed only when they change, one a frame
  while the panel's shown, and only when the port hasn't kept them
  (NitroPort_LowerMenuMapTextKept); the windows' tiles handed over as
  they are (the port makes them pixels).
